                         Imperium - Combat

Overview
========
Ships and planets can both carry weapons and fire on enemy ships and
planets with the FIRE command:

    fire <target> <attacker>

The target and the attacker can each be a ship (just the number, e.g. 12)
or a planet (the number followed by "p", e.g. 12p). For example:

    fire 7 3        ship 3 fires on ship 7
    fire 40p 3      ship 3 fires on planet 40
    fire 7 40p      planet 40 fires on ship 7
    fire 41p 40p    planet 40 fires on planet 41

You will be asked which weapon to use (if more than one kind is installed)
and how much to fire.

Firing on someone ends any alliance they had with you (it becomes
neutral), and neutral or default relations become war.

Weapons
=======
There are two kinds of weapons, both built with the BUILD command
(choose "weapon") using weapons production units:

  Blasers           Fire energy at a ship. Blasers have no effect on
                    planets. On a ship they use the ship's energy; on a
                    planet they are powered by ore (one ore per unit of
                    energy). Each blaser can safely discharge a certain
                    amount at once, depending on its tech level and
                    efficiency - firing more than that risks damaging the
                    ship or planet doing the firing, and far more than that
                    is not allowed.

  Photon torpedoes  One torpedo per tube, each using missiles from the
                    ship's hold or the planet. Launching costs ship energy
                    (on a ship) or mobility (on a planet). Torpedoes work
                    against both ships and planets, and are less accurate
                    the further away the target is.

A weapon only fires if it is installed (see INSTALL) and at least 60%
efficient. Installed weapons slowly become more efficient over time, and
their tech level slowly drops. Damage to a ship or planet also damages its
weapons, so a hard hit can put them out of action until they recover.

The range of a weapon depends on its tech level and efficiency.

Planet weapons
==============
Weapons can be installed on any planet you own (but not on your race's
shared home planet):

    build 40               build a weapon on planet 40
    install 40p 123        install weapon #123, which is on planet 40
    remove 40p 123         take it out again

A planet can have up to 8 weapons installed. An installed weapon can't be
loaded onto a ship or offered for sale until it is removed.

To fire, a planet needs:
  - weapons installed and at least 60% efficient
  - the planet itself at least 60% efficient
  - the same number of military and officers a ship would need to crew
    that kind of weapon
  - ore for blasers, or missiles and mobility for torpedoes

A planet aims using its own tech level and efficiency (it has no
computers like a ship does).

Defensive fire
==============
When you fire on someone, they get to fire back right away:

  1) If you fired on a planet, that planet fires back with full strength.
  2) If you fired on a ship, that ship fires back with full strength.
  3) Ships set to defend (see FLEET / the defend flag) that are in range
     of you help, using part of their weapons.
  4) Armed planets that are in range of you help, using part of their
     weapons.

Only ships and planets owned by the player you attacked, or by their
allies, take part - and never ones owned by you. Defensive fire stops as
soon as the attacker is destroyed. Blasers can't hurt planets, so a planet
that attacks is answered with torpedoes.

Damage
======
Ships are protected first by their shields, then their armour; what gets
through damages the ship, its cargo and its big items (installed or not).
If the hull is destroyed, so is the ship.

Planets have no shields or armour. A hit damages the planet's efficiency,
mobility, people, goods, production and the big items stored there
(including installed weapons). A planet whose civilians and military are
all killed becomes unowned.

Everyone involved is told what happened by telegram, and the attacks show
up in the news.

